course: everyday
lesson: 302
title: Safe toys and fair board games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Babies and toddlers explore the world by putting things in their mouths, so a small toy part that is harmless for you can choke a child under three.
explain: That is why toys carry age labels such as «3+», and why loose eyes, buttons, marbles, Lego bricks, balloons and long strings must be kept away from little ones.
explain: Board games and card games are fun when everyone plays fairly: shuffle well, deal honestly, keep the dice on the table and do not keep the others waiting too long.
explain: Example: a girl in Ohio sees a toy with a «3+» label in her baby brother's cot. She takes it out and gives him a big soft toy instead, because a small part could block his windpipe.
explain: Some games are won by thinking (chess) and some by luck (a simple dice game); in a game of chance nobody is to blame for losing.
fact: Small parts, marbles, Lego bricks and balloons can choke a child under three, so keep them away from babies and toddlers; little ones put things in their mouths and their airways are small.
fact: An age label such as «3+» warns that a toy can be dangerous for younger children, often because of small parts.
fact: Strings and cords can strangle, so keep them away from babies and never put a cord around anyone's neck.
fact: Ride-on toys and scooters should be kept away from pools and stairs, because a child can roll into the water or down the steps.
fact: A recalled toy has been found to be dangerous; tell a parent and stop using it until it is checked.
fact: A loose eye or button on a soft toy is a choking hazard; an adult should sew it on or remove it.
fact: Pretend play helps little children learn about the world, so let them lead the story and join in.
fact: In chess the aim is checkmate, trapping the king, not capturing as many pieces as possible.
fact: Fair shuffling and dealing, and not looking at others' cards, keep a card game honest.
fact: When a younger child wants to join a hard game, play on their team or choose a simpler game.
fact: Rolling dice inside a box lid or tray stops them getting lost and stops arguments about the number.
fact: In a game of chance, like snakes and ladders, luck decides the winner, so nobody should be blamed for losing.
fact: A good way to do a jigsaw is to find the edges and corners first, because edge pieces have a straight side and make a frame, then group pieces by colour.
exercise: A boy in Texas is building with small Lego bricks and his one-year-old sister crawls over. What should he do? | Move the small bricks out of her reach | Give her a few bricks to hold | Let her play with them, since she is right there next to him | Show her how to stack them up
exercise: Why are marbles and balloons dangerous for children under three? | They can get stuck in the throat and choke them | They are much too heavy for little hands to lift or hold up safely | Their bright colours hurt a baby's eyes | They make toddlers too excited to sleep
exercise: A toy box says «Not suitable for children under 3». What does that mean? | It may be dangerous for babies and toddlers, often because of small parts | It is too boring and too slow for little children to enjoy playing with | It is only for children who can already read | Toddlers would break it too quickly
exercise: A girl in Leeds wants to put a yo-yo string around her baby cousin's neck as a necklace. What is right? | Never put a cord around anyone's neck, because it can strangle | It is fine if the string is soft | It is fine if she ties a loose knot | It is fine for a minute or so, as long as she is watching the baby closely
exercise: Where should a toddler's ride-on car not be used? | Near a swimming pool or the top of the stairs | On a flat lawn in the back garden | In a big room with soft carpet | On a driveway with a parent watching nearby
exercise: Your family hears that a toy you own has been recalled. What do you do? | Tell a parent and stop using it until it is checked | Keep using it, but a bit more gently than before, so it won't break | Give it quickly to a younger friend | Ignore it because the toy still works
exercise: A teddy's plastic eye is hanging by a thread and a baby is about to play with it. What should you do? | Show an adult so they can sew it on or remove it | Pull it right off and leave it on the floor | Let the baby play with it, because teddies are always safe for babies | Glue it back on yourself with craft glue
exercise: Your little brother is playing doctor with a toy kit. What is the kindest way to join in? | Let him lead the story and play the patient | Take over and tell him he is doing it wrong | Tell him doctors do not really do that | Play your own game next to him instead
exercise: You are playing chess for the first time. What is the aim of the game? | To checkmate the other king | To capture all the other pieces | To get your pawns to the far side | To keep your queen safe all game
exercise: You are dealing cards for Uno with friends. What is fair? | Shuffle well, deal one at a time and do not look at the cards | Give yourself the best cards first | Peek at the cards as you deal them so you know exactly who has what | Let the person who is losing deal twice
exercise: Your six-year-old cousin wants to join a strategy game that is too hard for him. What could you do? | Let him be on your team or pick a simpler game | Tell him to go and play somewhere else | Let him play alone and lose every single round until he gives up | Let him join but ignore his moves
exercise: The dice keep rolling off the table during a game. What helps? | Roll them inside the box lid or a tray | Roll them harder so they stop sooner | Use only one die for the rest of the game | Let whoever picks them up choose the number
exercise: In a strategy game you have been thinking about one move for ten minutes. What is fair? | Think, but do not keep everyone waiting too long | Take as long as you like because winning matters | Make a random move without thinking at all | Ask the others to make your move for you
exercise: You lose a game where the winner is decided only by rolling dice. Whose fault is it? | Nobody's, because luck decided it | Yours, because you rolled badly | The winner's, because they cheated | The dice maker's, because the dice were bad
exercise: How do many people start a 500-piece jigsaw puzzle? | Find the edges and corners first, then group colours | Start in the middle and work outwards | Try every piece against every other piece until one of them fits | Put the pieces face down and guess
test: A family in Florida has a new baby and a four-year-old with a box of tiny toy beads. Which plan is wisest? | The four-year-old plays with beads only at a table the baby cannot reach | The baby can play with beads too, if they are big and brightly coloured | The beads stay on the floor so they are easy to find | The baby plays with beads only when it is not hungry
test: Which toy is safest to give a toddler? | A big soft ball with no small parts | A bag of marbles in many colours | A balloon that is not blown up | A small car with wheels that come off
test: A boy in Alaska ties a long cord to his baby brother's toy so he can pull it out of the cot. Why is this a bad idea? | The baby could get the cord around his neck and be strangled | The baby might pull the toy far too hard and hurt his arm with it | The cord will make the toy dirty | The toy will get lost under the cot
test: Which is true about a toy that has been recalled? | It was found to be dangerous and should not be used until checked | It is now worth more money to collectors | It just needs new batteries to work again | It is only dangerous for adults, not for the children who play with it
test: A girl in Sydney captures most of her friend's chess pieces but still loses the game. How can that be? | Her friend checkmated her king, which is what wins | Her friend had more pawns left at the end | Her friend took longer to think about each of her moves, which wins | Her friend moved the queen more times
test: Two children argue over whether a die that fell on the floor showed a six. What would have stopped the argument? | Rolling the die in a tray or box lid | Rolling the die more slowly in the air | Letting the oldest child decide every roll | Playing without dice for the rest of the day
test: Why might a jigsaw be easier if you start with the edges? | Edge pieces have a straight side, so they are quick to find and give a frame | Edge pieces are always the same colour | Edge pieces are bigger than the middle ones | The picture is always printed only on the edges of the box and the pieces
test: Your little sister is losing every round of a hard card game and starts to cry. What is kind and fair? | Play as a team with her or switch to a simpler game | Tell her she is far too young to play this game at all | Let her win by cheating for her secretly | Keep playing and say losing is her fault
test: A boy in Dublin loses a game of snakes and ladders and blames his friend. What should he understand? | It is a game of chance, so nobody is to blame | His friend must have cheated somehow | He would have won if he had thought harder about where to land | The board was unfair to the player in blue
test: Why do toddlers choke on small objects more than older children? | They put things in their mouths to explore, and their airways are small | They do not have any teeth yet | They do not like the taste of toys, so they spit them out too fast | They always eat too quickly at meals
test: my baby sis is 1, can she play with my lego | No, small parts can choke kids under three | Yes, lego is made for all ages | Yes, if she sits next to you | Only the big lego pieces she can lick and chew
test: teddy's eye is hanging off, toddler loves it | Get an adult to sew it on or take it off | Leave it, it's fine | Pull the eye off and give the teddy back | Glue it with school glue
test: chess - i took more pieces than him so i won right? | No, you win by checkmate, trapping the king | Yes, most pieces taken wins | Yes, if you took the queen | It's a draw if he has more pawns left at the end
test: lost at snakes n ladders, my mate says i played bad | No, it's luck, nobody's to blame for losing | Yes, you should have rolled better and thought more | Yes, it's all about skill | It's because you went second
explain: A baby in Ohio was given a toy marked «3+» by her big brother; she put a small piece in her mouth and choked, and their dad had to do back blows to save her.
explain: A toddler in Australia got his neck caught in a toy's long cord while playing alone; his mum found him just in time.
fact: Small toy parts, marbles and balloons are among the most common things young children choke on.
fact: Cords and strings longer than a short loop can strangle a baby or toddler.
test: can my 1yo cousin play with my lego | No, small parts can choke little ones | Yes, if you watch from across the room | Yes, the big pieces are fine | Yes, if she's careful
test: balloon popped, baby picking up the bits | Take them away; balloon pieces can choke | Leave them, they're soft | Let her chew one | Only the big pieces are dangerous
test: toy got recalled, it says on the news | Stop using it and tell a parent | Keep using it till it breaks | Give it to a younger child | Only worry if it's broken
test: did u hear a baby swallowed one of her big brother's lego bits and "went blue". lesson? | Keep small parts away from babies and toddlers | Lego is safe for babies | Only small babies choke | Big kids' toys are always fine for babies too
test: my mate says a toy got recalled cos it "exploded in a kid's hand". what's the bit to trust | A recalled toy is dangerous; stop using it and tell a parent | Recalled toys are fine to keep using if they look okay | Every recalled toy explodes | Recalls are just for old toys
test: kids say the boy who lost Monopoly every game is "a loser, rubbish at everything". pass it on? | No, luck decides chance games; don't spread it | Yes, he's a loser | Yes, and leave him out of the game next time | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an age label such as «3+» upon a toy? | It warns that the toy may be dangerous for younger children | It indicates that the toy is intended for three players or more | It indicates the toy's price in pounds | It shows the minimum number of batteries
exam: Identify the principal aim of a game of chess. | Checkmate, the trapping of the opposing king | The capture of as many of the opponent's pieces as possible | The occupation of every square | The removal of the opponent's queen
exam: It is given that a younger child wishes to join a difficult game. Select the response consistent with good sportsmanship. | One shall play upon their team or choose a simpler game | One shall exclude them from play | One shall allow them only to watch | One shall play harder in order to defeat them quickly
exam: State the purpose of rolling dice within a box lid or tray. | To prevent loss and disputes about the number | To make the numbers higher | To increase the chance of a six | To hide the result from the other players at the table
